7199982", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Zapatista Army of National Liberation, often referred to as the Zapatistas, is a far-left political and militant group that controls a substantial amount of territory in Chiapas, the southernmost state of Mexico.", "newValue": "The Zapatista Army of National Liberation (EZLN), founded on January 1, 1994, emerged as a significant revolutionary group in Chiapas, Mexico, advocating for indigenous rights and social justice in the face of neoliberal economic policies. The group gained international attention with their armed uprising against the Mexican government, coinciding with the implementation of the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA). Led by the charismatic figure Subcomandante Marcos, the Zapatistas utilized a combination of guerrilla tactics and political advocacy to highlight the struggles of the indigenous population while challenging the broader socio-economic disparities in Mexico.

Key events surrounding the Zapatista movement include the 1994 uprising in San Cristóbal de las Casas, where the EZLN seized towns, demanding land reform and autonomy for indigenous communities. The conflict drew widespread media coverage, prompting global solidarity movements and discussions about indigenous rights, economic justice, and anti-globalization. The Zapatistas declared war on the Mexican state, yet they also engaged in negotiations, culminating in the San Andrés Accords of 1996, which aimed to address indigenous rights and autonomy, though implementation has been uneven.

